---
/**
 * /alerts/unsubscribed/ — where the Worker lands a GET unsubscribe.
 *
 * By the time this renders the row is already deleted. The page also clears the
 * local sign-in token, because leaving a dead token behind would make the saved
 * page keep trying to sync against a subscription that no longer exists.
 */
import Base from '../../components/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---

<Base title="Unsubscribed" description="Your address has been removed from the alerts list.">
  <section class="masthead">
    <p class="eyebrow">Email alerts</p>
    <h1>You're unsubscribed</h1>
    <p class="lede">Your address and preferences have been deleted — not deactivated, deleted.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Nothing further will be sent. There is no record left to reactivate, so signing up again
      later starts from scratch (and needs a fresh confirmation click).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Your saved list is untouched.</strong> Starred workshops and papers live in this
      browser's own storage and always have — they are still on
      <a href={href('/saved/')}>your saved list</a>, and starring keeps working exactly as it does
      for anyone who never subscribed.
    </p>
    <p>
      If this was a mistake, you can <a href={href('/alerts/')}>subscribe again</a>. If the emails
      were simply too frequent, the manage page also has a <em>pause</em> option that keeps your
      preferences.
    </p>
  </div>

  <script is:inline>
    // The subscription is gone; a stale token here would only produce failing
    // sync requests on every page load. Shared helper, so the signup blocks
    // stop showing this browser as signed in.
    window.awtAlertsSignOut?.();
  </script>
</Base>
